Fig. 4b shows the Galvanostatic charge/discharge profiles for the different cycles at current densities of $200\mathrm{mA/g}$ and $2\mathrm{A/g}$ . For the first cycle at $200\mathrm{mA/g}$ , an obvious plateau at $\sim$ $0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ was observed in the first discharge curve, which was replaced by a slope from 1.5-0.9V in the second cycle, in accordance with the CV results. The electrode is able to deliver a high charge and discharge capacity of 1759 and $1144~\mathrm{mAh/g}$ , respectively. The irreversible capacity is believed to arise from the consumption of lithium ions during the formation of SEI film and incomplete re-oxidation of Ni metal.8
To fully activate the electrode, $200\mathrm{mA/g}$ was applied in the first five cycles and $2\mathrm{A/g}$ was carried out to run the long-cycle measurement. As shown in Fig. 4c, the NiO/Ni/Graphene composite electrode exhibited a high reversible capacity of $942{\mathrm{~mAh}}/{\mathrm{g}}$ at the beginning and then gradually increased to $1180\mathrm{mAh/g}$ after $\sim250$ cycles. This was usually explained by the gradual amorphization of crystalline NiO, which produced more Li ion accessible sites and improved kinetics for re-oxidation of Ni to NiO.43, 46 The reversible capacity after 1000 cycles still reaches $962\mathrm{\mAh/g}$ , slightly higher than the initial value. To the best of our knowledge, this is the best cycle performance ever reported about a NiO based electrode. (See Table S1)
To investigate the structure stability after the cycling test, the coin cell was disassembled after one thousand charge-discharge cycles. As shown in the optical image (Fig. S8a), the electrode remained intact. TEM image (Fig. S8b) of the NiO/Ni/Graphene composites after one thousand cycles showed that the original morphology of the electrode materials was preserved, indicating a high stability of the active materials in the LIBs.
Rate capability is of pivotal importance for many applications of LIBs. Cycle performance
## ACS Nano
at different current densities was studied and the results are summarized in Fig. 4d.  When cycled at $200\mathrm{mA/g},500\mathrm{mA/g},1\mathrm{A/g},2\mathrm{A/g},4\mathrm{A/g},$ and $8\mathrm{A/g}$ , the electrode delivered an average capacity of $1127~\mathrm{mAh/g}$ , 1162 mAh/g, 1170 mAh/g, 1143 mAh/g, 1120 mAh/g, and $1030\mathrm{mAh/g}$ , respectively. The reversible capacity was almost unchanged even when the current density was increased to $8~\mathrm{A/g}$ . This exceptional lithium ion and electron mobility can be attributed to the unique structure obtained from the Ni-MOF. Even under the current density of 15 $\operatorname{A}/\operatorname{g}.$ , which means the battery can finish a complete charge-discharge process within seven minutes, the NiO/Ni/Graphene composite electrode still possessed an average capacity of $805\mathrm{mAh/g}$ , much higher than the previous reports on NiO based electrode (Fig. 4e).47-49 Importantly, when the current density was reduced back to $200\mathrm{mA/g}$ , the specific capacity was recovered to the original value.
The sodium storage behavior of hierarchical hollow NiO/Ni/Graphene composites was also investigated. The sodium ion battery is an attractive low-cost solution for rechargeable electrochemical energy storage system. However, the Na ion ( $0.102{\mathrm{nm}}$ in radius) is much larger than the Li ion $0.059\mathrm{nm}$ in radius), resulting in more severe volume expansion and much lower kinetics in conversion reactions. It is reported that transitional metal oxides can be used for SIBs as well and the strategies for LIBs can be extended to the SIBs. Nanostructure engineering and carbon hybridization are typically required for transition metal oxide ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ , SnO, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{CuO})^{50-55}$ anodes for SIBs. As far as we know, the research of NiO anode in SIBs is limited49 and no report of MOFs derived NiO/Ni composites for SIBs has been published.
The mechanism for sodium storage is similar to lithium storage, as shown in Fig. 5a, the CV of the electrode was measured at the same condition for the lithium ion battery. During
## ACS Nano
the first cathodic scan process, two intense peaks at 0.8 and $0.5\mathrm{V}$ could be assigned to the reduction of NiO and formation of SEI film, respectively. For the anodic scan, the strong peak at $1.6\mathrm{V}$ could be ascribed to the re-oxidation of metallic Ni.49 In addition, a small peak at $0.5\mathrm{V}$ was observed, which might originate from partial decomposition of SEI film, similar to the LIBs. Based on this result, the overall reaction for SIB is concluded as:
$$
\mathrm{NiO}+2\mathrm{Na}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\Leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
